# Exploring Datasets

```elixir
Mix.install([
  # To try a local checkout use {:eurostatx, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")} instead
  {:eurostatx, "~> 0.1"},
  {:kino_explorer, "~> 0.1"}
])
```

## Introduction

This notebook shows how to find datasets, understand their structure and query them with the
SDMX APIs, in any format. See the *Getting Started* notebook for the basics.

```elixir
alias Explorer.DataFrame, as: DF

Eurostatx.Telemetry.Logger.attach(level: :info)
```

## The Table of Contents

The table of contents is Eurostat's navigation tree: folders, datasets and pre-defined tables, with
their last update, time coverage and number of values.

```elixir
{:ok, toc} = Eurostatx.Catalogue.toc()

toc |> Enum.take(15) |> Eurostatx.DataFrame.from_records()
```

`Eurostatx.Catalogue.search/2` finds datasets and tables by code or title. Pass the table of
contents to avoid downloading it again. This is how we'd find `une_rt_m`, the monthly unemployment
rate of the [demo](https://jaimeiniesta.com/en/eurostatx/jobs):

```elixir
{:ok, results} = Eurostatx.Catalogue.search("unemployment", toc: toc)

results
|> Eurostatx.DataFrame.from_records()
|> DF.select(["code", "title", "type", "last_update", "data_start", "data_end", "values"])
```

## Every Dataset

`Eurostatx.Datasets.list/1` lists every dataset with Eurostat's annotations: number of
observations, oldest and latest periods, date of the last update...

```elixir
{:ok, datasets} = Eurostatx.Datasets.list()

datasets_df =
  datasets
  |> Eurostatx.DataFrame.from_records()
  |> DF.select(["id", "label", "type", "observations", "oldest_period", "latest_period", "data_updated_at"])
```

For example, the datasets updated most recently:

```elixir
datasets_df
|> DF.sort_with(&[desc: &1["data_updated_at"]])
|> DF.head(10)
```

## The Structure of a Dataset

Let's explore `une_rt_m`, the monthly unemployment rate. Its **data structure** lists the
dimensions in the order of the series keys, the time dimension and the attributes (such as the
observation flags):

```elixir
{:ok, structure} = Eurostatx.Datasets.structure("une_rt_m")

structure["dimensions"] |> Enum.map(&Map.take(&1, ["position", "id"]))
```

`Eurostatx.Datasets.values/2` gives the codes used by each dimension, with their labels:

```elixir
{:ok, dimensions} = Eurostatx.Datasets.values("une_rt_m")

for dimension <- dimensions, value <- dimension["values"] do
  %{dimension: dimension["id"], code: value["code"], name: value["name"]}
end
|> DF.new()
```

And `Eurostatx.Datasets.availability/2` the codes and time periods actually available:

```elixir
{:ok, availability} = Eurostatx.Datasets.availability("une_rt_m")

{List.first(availability["TIME_PERIOD"]), List.last(availability["TIME_PERIOD"])}
```

## Code Lists

The labels come from **code lists**, shared by many datasets. They may have more codes than the
ones used by a dataset:

```elixir
{:ok, codes} = Eurostatx.Codelists.codes("sex", language: :fr)
codes
```

## SDMX Data Queries

`Eurostatx.Data` queries the SDMX 3.0 API (or SDMX 2.1 with `sdmx_version: "2.1"`). Filters work
like in the Statistics API, and the response can be JSON-stat (the default), SDMX-CSV, TSV or
SDMX-ML. `last_n` returns the last observations of each series:

```elixir
Eurostatx.Data.get_as_data_frame!("une_rt_m",
  filters: [geo: ["EU27_2020", "ES", "DE"], age: "TOTAL", sex: "T", unit: "PC_ACT", s_adj: "SA"],
  last_n: 3,
  labels: true
)
```

Series can also be selected with a **key**: the codes of the dimensions in the order of the data
structure (`freq.s_adj.age.unit.sex.geo`). Missing trailing dimensions mean "all":

```elixir
{:ok, csv} = Eurostatx.Data.get("une_rt_m", key: "M.SA.TOTAL.PC_ACT.T.ES", last_n: 3, format: :csv)
IO.puts(csv)
```

SDMX-CSV can be loaded straight into Explorer, which is the fastest way to handle big extractions:

```elixir
Eurostatx.DataFrame.from_csv(csv)
```

The Eurostat TSV format, inherited from the former bulk download, has one line per series:

```elixir
{:ok, tsv} =
  Eurostatx.Data.get("une_rt_m",
    filters: [geo: ["ES", "DE"], age: "TOTAL", sex: "T", unit: "PC_ACT", s_adj: "SA"],
    start_period: "2026-01",
    format: :tsv
  )

IO.puts(tsv)
Eurostatx.DataFrame.from_tsv(tsv)
```

## Latest Updates

The RSS feed lists the datasets and code lists created, updated or deleted in the last 7 days:

```elixir
{:ok, %{"items" => items}} = Eurostatx.Catalogue.rss()

items |> Eurostatx.DataFrame.from_records() |> DF.select(["pub_date", "code", "category", "description"])
```

## Big Extractions

Extractions above 500,000 cells are processed asynchronously by Eurostat, and Eurostatx waits for
them transparently. Eurostat asks to make **one request at a time**, and Eurostatx never makes
concurrent requests.
